#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161729 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a9a1aa0-8822-5dbd-8534-254ace55c555" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Canonical Form of a Quadratic Function by Finding Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "ce37b130-8aa9-58af-b3b8-bf78b5a741e7" :question/math-academy-id "e-8249" :question/problem "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2+x_2^2+2x_3^2+4x_1x_2$." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid A - λI \\mid &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 - λ & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 - λ & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(2 - λ)\\begin{vmatrix}1 - λ & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(2 - λ)((1 - λ)(1 - λ) - 4) &= 0 \\\\\n(2 - λ)(λ^{2} - 2λ - 3) &= 0 \\\\\n(2 - λ)(λ - 3)(λ + 1) &= 0 \\\\\nλ &= 3, 2,-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3y_1^2 + 2y_2^2 - y_3^2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161730 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1bee282-2775-59f5-a929-9dc5327703bc" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Function Over the 2D-Space to the Canonical Form Using Orthogonal Transformations"}] :question/id #uuid "c8ec4ba0-e0bf-5842-8f5e-4914ce46706a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8250" :question/problem "Given the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=2x_1^2-2x_1x_2+2x_2^2$, find its canonical (diagonal) form $\\mathbf{y}^T \\! D \\mathbf{y}$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $\\mathbf{x}=P\\mathbf{y}$ defines the corresponding change of variables." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid A - λI \\mid &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 - λ & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} - 4λ + 3 &= 0 \\\\\nλ &= 3, 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A$.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=3$, we have\n$A - 3I|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} - 3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1 \\| = \\sqrt{2}$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- For $\\lambda_2=1$, we have\n$A - I|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2 \\| = \\sqrt{2}$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161731 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07cbbfc-0f61-55e8-afe9-b675dc74f24e" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Function Over the 3D-Space to the Canonical Form Using Orthogonal Transformations"}] :question/id #uuid "243d1a75-6f0f-5d55-a7ec-333487fa37bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-8251" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $\\mathbf{x}=P\\mathbf{y}$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}$ to the canonical (diagonal) form $\\mathbf{y}^T \\! D \\mathbf{y}$." :question/worked-solution "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=4, \\quad\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=2, \\quad\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\quad\\lambda_3=1\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A$.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=4$, we have\n$A - 4I|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} - 4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1 \\| = \\sqrt{2}$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- For $\\lambda_2=2$, we have\n$A - 2I|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} - 2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2 \\| = \\sqrt{2}$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- For $\\lambda_3=1$, we have\n$A - I|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_3$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_3 \\| = 1$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162556 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a9a1aa0-8822-5dbd-8534-254ace55c555" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Canonical Form of a Quadratic Function by Finding Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db1dbf4d-28a2-5d5a-87a4-265ea6b7fe91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y_{1}^{2} + 2y_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160969} {:answer/id #uuid "f3223822-6f0e-5c31-80a6-7a560ccb85bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y_{1}^{2} - 2y_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160970} {:answer/id #uuid "0c65c312-10f2-5464-b5eb-0a8a5c4a24c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3y_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160971} {:answer/id #uuid "663da8b7-3410-5291-868a-05b65163a3d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2y_{1}^{2} - 3y_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160972} {:answer/id #uuid "7d778a3b-8dc1-53d9-9b9b-c2dcb5d280a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y_{1}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3223822-6f0e-5c31-80a6-7a560ccb85bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y_{1}^{2} - 2y_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160970} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4ddf5b3-0488-55e6-bb51-ec7b4aa70d5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160968}] :question/id #uuid "e7899e1b-4eee-518b-aa53-ed72b98bf89e" :question/math-academy-id "q-94446" :question/problem "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x) =-x_{1}^{2} + 4x_{1}x_{2} + 2x_{2}^{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a9a1aa0-8822-5dbd-8534-254ace55c555" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Canonical Form of a Quadratic Function by Finding Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "807ea595-f5a3-5281-a2c3-4ef489f95228" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{1}^{2} - y_{2}^{2} + 2y_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160975} {:answer/id #uuid "f9e9f576-127f-56fa-ae95-3359c5cac537" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y_{1}^{2} - 2y_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160976} {:answer/id #uuid "2d71aad6-66bd-5ff8-90f1-cd7b1255a767" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y_{1}^{2} - y_{2}^{2} - 2y_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160977} {:answer/id #uuid "5557225a-dee8-5411-bf7e-482a3aa01bbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-y_{1}^{2} - 2y_{2}^{2} - 2y_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160978} {:answer/id #uuid "6ac6eceb-17ce-55e9-bcf7-bc9866d04ac1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y_{1}^{2} + y_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d71aad6-66bd-5ff8-90f1-cd7b1255a767" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y_{1}^{2} - y_{2}^{2} - 2y_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160977} :answer-field/id #uuid "0785eadd-8f77-5860-b9ab-0d2b5b750918" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160974}] :question/id #uuid "72599bcc-35e4-5761-98ef-23a9eae51976" :question/math-academy-id "q-94449" :question/problem "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x) = x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2} - x_{3}^{2} - 6x_{1}x_{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162559 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07cbbfc-0f61-55e8-afe9-b675dc74f24e" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Function Over the 3D-Space to the Canonical Form Using Orthogonal Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93afd1e7-589c-572d-81e8-cd95c2c7e1a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160987} {:answer/id #uuid "b5ead3be-52cb-51bc-a4ec-b140d0258480" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160988} {:answer/id #uuid "ca5ddbfb-38cf-5614-b107-bae7c3df3a66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160989} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce52d2a-928e-513a-97af-4fe0bcce819b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160990} {:answer/id #uuid "5809b31c-60e6-5689-a138-5e6ee5628d38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5ead3be-52cb-51bc-a4ec-b140d0258480" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160988} :answer-field/id #uuid "a39de5df-822b-5722-89ab-814c839cfed2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160986}] :question/id #uuid "7f0148f4-83df-5e99-8325-e264016c452b" :question/math-academy-id "q-94461" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}, D = \\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x = Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(x) = x^{T}Ax$ to the canonical (diagonal) form $y^{T}Dy$."}]}
